<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Lichtensteig</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Lichtensteig is a small town in the canton of St Gallen, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/switzerland">Switzerland</a>, on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thur">Thur</a> in the Toggenburg valley. Founded by the counts of Toggenburg in the 13th century below their castle, it was the chief town of the valley, and after the Abbey of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saint-gall">Saint Gall</a> bought the county in 1468 it became the seat of the abbey's governors.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/lichtensteig.jpg" alt="Lichtensteig" width="320" height="212" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: böhringer friedrich · CC BY-SA 2.5 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Lichtensteig_2.JPG" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Lichtensteig lies in the Toggenburg, the upper basin of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thur">Thur</a>, on the right bank of the river. It is downstream of Wattwil and upstream of Dietfurt, west of the Wasserfluh Pass. The ruins of Neu-Toggenburg castle stand on a rocky outcrop above the village. With only 2.8 square kilometres, it is the smallest municipality of Toggenburg by area, and its height ranges from 591 to 1,146 metres. It had 1,879 inhabitants in 2020, and most speak German.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>The counts of Toggenburg founded Lichtensteig in the early 13th century, and it is first mentioned in 1228 as Liehtunsteige. A market is mentioned in 1374, and the right to hold markets was confirmed in 1400. A letter of privileges of the lords of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/raron">Raron</a> in 1439 confirms a council of twelve <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/burghers">burghers</a> and the joint appointment of judges by burghers and landlords; the mayor was appointed by the landlord from a list drawn up by the burghers. After the Abbey of St Gallen acquired the Toggenburg in 1468, Lichtensteig became the seat of the abbot's reeve, and in 1469 Abbot <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ulrich-rosch">Ulrich Rösch</a> confirmed its privileges.</p><p>The council declared for the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/protestant-reformation">Reformation</a> in 1528. The church was used by both Reformed and Catholics, while schools were separated by confession until 1868, when a neo-Gothic church was built that both confessions still used until two new churches were built in 1967 and 1970. The town grew in importance as a market town in the 19th century with the textile home-working industry of the Toggenburg. In the early 20th century it had six yearly markets and a weekly livestock market. The railway reached it in 1870, and in 1874 the settlements of Blatten, Hof and Loretto were added to it.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>The Old Town Council building at Hintergasse 22 is listed as a Swiss heritage site of national significance, and the whole village is part of the Inventory of Swiss Heritage Sites. The clockmaker, instrument maker and mathematician Jost Bürgi was born in Lichtensteig in 1552. The municipal coat of arms, a letter L in gold on a field divided red and black, was adopted in 1946 from elements in use since the 16th century.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Lichtensteig” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The lords of Toggenburg appear in the 11th century and are documented as counts by the early 13th century, holding the upper Thur valley from castles near <b>Lichtensteig</b>.</blockquote><a href="/state/county-of-toggenburg">County of Toggenburg</a></li></ul></section>
